  updatedLabel={UPDATED_LABEL}
  article={{ headline: 'Chain-Link Fence in St. George: Cost, Coatings, and Where It Is Allowed', datePublished: UPDATED }}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { href: '/fence-installation-st-george-ut/', label: 'Fence installation hub', blurb: 'All materials, heights, and permit rules.' },
    { href: '/fence-and-block-wall-cost-st-george-ut/', label: 'Full cost guide', blurb: 'Every range and source.' },
    { href: '/vinyl-fence-installation-st-george-ut/', label: 'Vinyl fence guide', blurb: 'The privacy alternative.' },
    { href: '/wood-privacy-fence-st-george-ut/', label: 'Wood privacy fence guide', blurb: 'Lowest-cost solid privacy.' },
    { href: '/fence-repair-st-george-ut/', label: 'Fence repair', blurb: 'Sagging mesh, leaning posts, bent gates.' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Chain-link is the least expensive fence you can put around a St. George lot, and in the desert it has a quiet advantage
    over wood: galvanized steel does not shrink, check, or gray in the sun. What it does not do is give privacy, and what it
    does not always pass is the HOA. This guide covers what a chain-link fence costs in St. George, the difference between
    galvanized and coated mesh, what to specify for heat, irrigation, and wind, where the city allows it and at what height,
    the slats question, and how to compare quotes from independent fence companies.
  </p>

  <h2>What a chain-link fence costs in St. George</h2>
  <CostTable
    caption={`Chain-link fence cost, as of ${COST_AS_OF}`}
    unit="per linear foot, installed"
    rows={[
      { label: 'Chain-link, national range', range: chain.range, note: chain.note },
      { label: 'Chain-link, 4-foot, St. George (HomeBlue, June 2022)', range: localCheck2022.chainLink, note: 'Older local estimate; cross-check only.' },
      { label: 'Galvanized (HomeGuide)', range: chainLink.galvanized, note: 'Standard silver mesh and framework.' },
      { label: 'Vinyl-coated, black or green (HomeGuide)', range: chainLink.vinylCoated, note: `Custom colors add a ${chainLink.colorPremium} premium.` },
      { label: '4-foot height (HomeGuide)', range: chainLink.height4ft, note: 'The front-yard maximum in most St. George zones.' },
      { label: '6-foot height (HomeGuide)', range: chainLink.height6ft, note: 'Taller mesh, heavier posts, more concrete.' },
      { label: 'Privacy slats, added (HomeGuide)', range: chainLink.slats, note: 'Turns open mesh into a wind-catching panel; see below.' },
      { label: 'Wood privacy, for comparison', range: wood.range, note: 'Needs sealing every 1 to 2 years in this climate.' },
      { label: 'Vinyl (PVC) privacy, for comparison', range: vinyl.range, note: 'Solid privacy, no finish.' },
    ]}
  />
  <p>
    Three things move the price inside that range: coating (coated mesh costs well over galvanized), height (a 6-foot fence
    uses more mesh and heavier posts than a 4-foot one), and gates, which HomeGuide prices separately at {chainLink.walkGate}
    for a walk gate and {chainLink.driveGate} for a double driveway gate before installation. Gauge matters too, with 9-gauge
    mesh costing more than the lighter residential 11 or 11.5 gauge. If an old fence has to come out first, HomeGuide puts
    removal at {chainLink.removal} per linear foot. No St. George fence company publishes prices, so these are dated published
    ranges, not local quotes; the <a href="/fence-and-block-wall-cost-st-george-ut/">cost guide</a> lists every source and
    date. For a full perimeter, chain-link at {chain.range} per foot against ornamental iron at {iron.range} is the gap that
    keeps it the default on larger lots.
  </p>

  <h2>Galvanized, coated, gauge: what you are buying</h2>
  <IconList items={mesh} cols={2} />
  <p>
    The common St. George combination for a visible fence is black vinyl-coated mesh on black framework, 11-gauge, 2-inch
    diamond, 4 feet in front and up to 6 feet in back. Against red rock, desert plantings, and a dark house trim it reads as
    a thin line rather than a fence. Plain galvanized is the right call where the fence will not be seen from the street or
    where the budget is the whole point, and it lasts as long or longer because there is no coating to fail. Whatever the
    choice, the gauge and the post sizes decide how the fence feels in five years, not the color.
  </p>

  <h2>Heat, irrigation, and wind: what to specify</h2>
  <p>
    St. George tied Utah's all-time high of 117 degrees on July 10, 2021, a reading NOAA's State Climate Extremes Committee
    formally accepted, and ordinary summers run above 100 for weeks. Steel shrugs that off; a galvanized fence here will
    outlive a wood fence that was never sealed. The desert does find chain-link's weak points, though. Cheap polymer
    coatings chalk and crack under years of ultraviolet light. Hollow posts left uncapped fill with sprinkler water and rust
    from the inside out. And the mesh that is sprayed every morning by the irrigation line and bakes dry every afternoon is
    where corrosion shows first. None of that rules chain-link out; it makes the specification the decision.
  </p>
  <IconList items={desertSpec} cols={2} />
  <p>
    Wind is the other design load, and chain-link is unusual in that it can go either way. Open mesh lets wind through, which
    is why a well-set chain-link fence survives a storm that blows wood panels out of their rails. Add privacy slats or a
    windscreen and the same fence becomes a solid panel on posts that were sized for an open one. The
    <a href="/fence-repair-st-george-ut/">repair guide</a> describes what that looks like after the fact: mesh sagging where
    posts lean and the tension wire has loosened. If slats are in the plan, say so before the post holes are dug.
  </p>

  <h2>Where chain-link makes sense in St. George</h2>
  <IconList items={uses} />

  <h2>Height, openness, and permits under city code</h2>
  <p>
    Height and openness are governed by St. George City Code
    <a href="https://stgeorge.municipal.codes/Code/10-18-1" rel="noopener">§10-18-1</a>. In most zones, front-yard fences are
    capped at 4 feet and side and rear-yard fences at 6 feet 4 inches, and within 10 feet behind the sidewalk only decorative
    fences that are at least 50 percent open are permitted. Bare chain-link is open mesh, so a 4-foot fence along the street
    can meet that rule where a solid privacy panel cannot. Slats change the answer: a slatted fence is no longer 50 percent
    open, so keep slats to side and rear runs or to the part of the lot behind the 10-foot line. Under
    <a href="https://stgeorge.municipal.codes/Code/10-18-5" rel="noopener">§10-18-5</a>, any fence in a front setback needs a
    permit; the <a href="/fence-permit-st-george-ut/">permit guide</a> covers the application. Washington, Hurricane, Ivins,
    and Santa Clara each have their own code; see the <a href="/washington-county-fence-service-areas/">service area guide</a>.
    Before any post holes are dug, the company should call Blue Stakes of Utah to locate buried utilities.
  </p>

  <h2>HOA rules: the real limit on chain-link</h2>
  <p>
    Chain-link is the material St. George HOAs restrict most often. Many newer subdivisions prohibit it outright on any lot
    line visible from a street, golf course, or open space, and some prohibit it everywhere. Where it is allowed, the typical
    condition is black or green vinyl-coated mesh with matching framework, a height cap, and no slats. It varies by HOA, so
    confirm your own CC&amp;Rs and get the HOA's written fence standard before you get quotes. The
    <a href="/hoa-fence-requirements-st-george-ut/">HOA fence guide</a> explains how HOA rules sit on top of city code and
    what the approval process usually involves. On an older lot in town with no HOA, the city code above is the only rule.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy slats and windscreens</h2>
  <p>
    Slats are the usual answer when a chain-link budget meets a wish for privacy. HomeGuide prices them at {chainLink.slats}
    per linear foot added to the fence, and they come in vinyl or aluminum, in colors, and in styles from simple vertical
    slats to woven or hedge-style inserts. Two cautions apply here more than elsewhere. First, the wind load described above:
    slats should be planned before the posts are set. Second, sun: inexpensive slats fade and go brittle after a few St.
    George summers, so ask for a UV-rated product. If privacy is the main goal from the start, compare the slatted total
    against the <a href="/wood-privacy-fence-st-george-ut/">wood privacy fence</a> and
    <a href="/vinyl-fence-installation-st-george-ut/">vinyl fence</a> guides, since a slatted chain-link fence can approach
    the price of a wood fence that was built for privacy.
  </p>

  <h2>Lifespan and upkeep: chain-link against the alternatives</h2>
  <p>
    HomeGuide estimates {chainLink.lifespan} for a chain-link fence. That sits below Angi's {lifespan.wood.range} for wood
    and {lifespan.vinyl.range} for vinyl on paper, but the comparison hides the upkeep. Wood reaches its long end only with
    sealing every 1 to 2 years; chain-link needs almost nothing beyond re-tensioning the mesh, re-hanging a gate, and keeping
    sprinklers off the fence line. In practice a galvanized fence on well-set, capped posts often outlasts the published
    range in dry climates, and a coated fence lives as long as its coating. What ends a chain-link fence is usually damage,
    not age: a vehicle, a fallen limb, or a post that rusted at the ground.
  </p>

  <h2>Repair or replace an older chain-link fence?</h2>
  <p>
    Chain-link is the most repairable fence material. A sagging run is usually a loose tension wire or a leaning post, a bent
    section can be cut out and re-woven, and a gate that drags is re-hung. HomeGuide's published repair ranges start at
    {repair.minor} for minor work, with a single post replacement around {repair.post} and gate repair at {repair.gate}. When
    several posts have rusted through at the ground or the mesh is rusting along its whole length, a new fence on new posts is
    the better spend. The <a href="/fence-repair-st-george-ut/">fence repair guide</a> walks through the repair-versus-replace
    decision and what each type of damage looks like.
  </p>

  <h2>How to compare chain-link quotes</h2>
  <IconList items={compareSteps} />
  <p>
    Confirm the company's Utah license status with the
    <a href="https://secure.utah.gov/llv/search/index.html" rel="noopener">DOPL license lookup</a> and ask for proof of
    insurance; the <a href="/licensed-fence-contractor-compare-st-george-ut/">license guide</a> explains what the record
    shows and the small-project exemption, which a short chain-link job can fall under. If you want help finding independent
    local companies to quote, the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> describes how matching works.
  </p>

  <p class="sgfw-note">
    Sources: NerdWallet, <a href="https://www.nerdwallet.com/home-ownership/home-improvement/learn/cost-to-install-a-fence" rel="noopener">"Cost to Install a Fence"</a> (updated July 10, 2025);
    HomeGuide, <a href={chainLink.url} rel="noopener">"How Much Does a Chain-Link Fence Cost?"</a> ({chainLink.date}; read October 14, 2026) and
    <a href="https://homeguide.com/costs/fence-repair-cost" rel="noopener">"Fence Repair Cost"</a> (updated February 20, 2026);
    HomeBlue, <a href="https://www.homeblue.com/fence-installation/saint-george-ut-fence-cost.htm" rel="noopener">"Fence Cost in Saint George, Utah"</a> (June 28, 2022; cross-check only);
    Angi, <a href={lifespan.wood.url} rel="noopener">"How Long Does a Wood Fence Last?"</a> ({lifespan.wood.date}) and
    <a href={lifespan.vinyl.url} rel="noopener">"How Long Does a Vinyl Fence Last?"</a> ({lifespan.vinyl.date});
    NOAA NCEI, <a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/monitoring-content/extremes/scec/reports/20220708-Utah-Maximum-Temperature.pdf" rel="noopener">State Climate Extremes Committee report, Utah maximum temperature</a> (July 2022);
    Blue Stakes of Utah, <a href="https://www.bluestakes.org/" rel="noopener">bluestakes.org</a>;
    St. George City Code <a href="https://stgeorge.municipal.codes/Code/10-18-1" rel="noopener">§10-18-1</a> and
    <a href="https://stgeorge.municipal.codes/Code/10-18-5" rel="noopener">§10-18-5</a> (checked September 26, 2026).
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
